WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:02.026 --> 00:05.786 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference November 7,

00:06.106 --> 00:09.726 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:09.786 --> 00:10.486 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:11.366 --> 00:13.576 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:13.576 --> 00:16.846 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:16.846 --> 00:19.466 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:20.856 --> 00:22.346 align:middle
The economy is strong overall

00:22.346 --> 00:24.746 align:middle
and has made significant
progress toward our goals

00:24.856 --> 00:26.196 align:middle
over the past two years.

00:27.496 --> 00:28.676 align:middle
The labor market has cooled

00:28.676 --> 00:31.566 align:middle
from its formerly overheated
state and remains solid.

00:33.686 --> 00:35.966 align:middle
Inflation has eased
substantially from a peak

00:35.966 --> 00:39.066 align:middle
of 7 percent to 2.1
percent as of September.

00:40.706 --> 00:43.006 align:middle
We are committed to maintaining
our economy's strength

00:43.286 --> 00:46.466 align:middle
by supporting maximum employment
and returning inflation

00:46.466 --> 00:47.646 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal.

00:49.966 --> 00:53.036 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided
to take another step

00:53.206 --> 00:55.306 align:middle
in reducing the degree
of policy restraint

00:55.976 --> 00:58.976 align:middle
by lowering our policy interest
rate by ¼ percentage point.

01:01.076 --> 01:02.646 align:middle
We continue to be
confident that,

01:02.646 --> 01:04.436 align:middle
with an appropriate
recalibration

01:04.436 --> 01:07.486 align:middle
of our policy stance,
strength in the economy

01:07.486 --> 01:09.196 align:middle
and the labor market
can be maintained,

01:09.766 --> 01:12.216 align:middle
with inflation moving
sustainably down to 2 percent.

01:13.736 --> 01:17.006 align:middle
We also decided to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

01:18.026 --> 01:20.166 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

01:20.166 --> 01:22.976 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:25.446 --> 01:28.696 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:28.866 --> 01:30.726 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

01:32.086 --> 01:35.086 align:middle
GDP rose at an annual
rate of 2.8 percent

01:35.086 --> 01:37.976 align:middle
in the third quarter,
about the same pace

01:37.976 --> 01:38.866 align:middle
as in the second quarter.

01:40.096 --> 01:42.546 align:middle
Growth of consumer spending
has remained resilient,

01:43.276 --> 01:46.276 align:middle
and investment in equipment and
intangibles has strengthened.

01:47.716 --> 01:50.396 align:middle
In contrast, activity in the
housing sector has been weak.

01:51.646 --> 01:54.626 align:middle
Overall, improving supply
conditions have supported the

01:54.626 --> 01:57.376 align:middle
strong performance of the U.S.
economy over the past year.

01:59.796 --> 02:01.896 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions remain solid.

02:02.616 --> 02:05.536 align:middle
Payroll job gains have slowed
from earlier in the year,

02:05.676 --> 02:09.896 align:middle
averaging 104,000 per month
over the past three months.

02:11.156 --> 02:13.346 align:middle
This figure would have been
somewhat higher were it not

02:13.346 --> 02:15.706 align:middle
for the effects of labor
strikes and hurricanes

02:15.766 --> 02:16.996 align:middle
on employment in October.

02:18.336 --> 02:21.716 align:middle
Regarding the hurricanes,
let me extend our sympathies

02:21.716 --> 02:24.236 align:middle
to all the families,
businesses, and communities

02:24.726 --> 02:27.546 align:middle
who have been harm-harmed
by these devastating storms.

02:30.006 --> 02:32.716 align:middle
The unemployment rate is notably
higher than it was a year ago

02:32.936 --> 02:36.086 align:middle
but has edged down over the past
three months and remains low

02:36.306 --> 02:38.156 align:middle
at 4.1 percent in October.

02:39.636 --> 02:42.096 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

02:42.276 --> 02:44.346 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

02:46.056 --> 02:48.486 align:middle
Overall, a broad set
of indicators suggests

02:48.486 --> 02:51.256 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are now less tight

02:51.326 --> 02:53.996 align:middle
than just before the
pandemic in 2019.

02:55.406 --> 02:56.966 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:56.966 --> 02:58.886 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

03:01.566 --> 03:03.136 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly

03:03.136 --> 03:04.326 align:middle
over the past two years.

03:04.946 --> 03:08.546 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.1 percent

03:08.546 --> 03:10.406 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in September;

03:11.116 --> 03:13.666 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:14.956 --> 03:17.316 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.7 percent.

03:18.826 --> 03:20.846 align:middle
Overall, inflation
has moved much closer

03:20.846 --> 03:22.826 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal,

03:23.526 --> 03:25.686 align:middle
but core inflation
remains somewhat elevated.

03:27.306 --> 03:29.606 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

03:29.606 --> 03:32.756 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

03:32.756 --> 03:35.366 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

03:36.116 --> 03:38.076 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

03:40.716 --> 03:43.836 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:43.836 --> 03:46.446 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:46.446 --> 03:47.436 align:middle
for the American people.

03:48.726 --> 03:50.586 align:middle
We see the risks to
achieving our employment

03:50.586 --> 03:53.006 align:middle
and inflation goals as
being roughly in balance,

03:53.466 --> 03:56.546 align:middle
and we're attentive to the risks
to both sides of our mandate.

03:57.746 --> 04:00.036 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

04:00.036 --> 04:02.196 align:middle
to lower the target range
for the federal funds rate

04:02.296 --> 04:07.096 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point, to
4½ percent to 4  percent.

04:08.216 --> 04:09.726 align:middle
This further recalibration

04:09.726 --> 04:12.956 align:middle
of our policy stance will
help maintain the strength

04:12.956 --> 04:15.796 align:middle
of the economy and the labor
market and will continue

04:15.796 --> 04:17.806 align:middle
to enable further
progress on inflation

04:18.176 --> 04:21.036 align:middle
as we move toward a more
neutral stance over time.

04:23.026 --> 04:25.986 align:middle
We know that reducing policy
strain-restraint too quickly

04:25.986 --> 04:27.556 align:middle
could hinder progress
on inflation.

04:28.376 --> 04:31.176 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too slowly

04:31.426 --> 04:34.086 align:middle
could unduly weaken economic
activity and employment.

04:34.976 --> 04:38.116 align:middle
In considering additional
adjustments to the target range

04:38.116 --> 04:39.196 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

04:39.786 --> 04:42.126 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess incoming data,

04:42.446 --> 04:44.676 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

04:45.426 --> 04:47.336 align:middle
We are not on any preset course.

04:48.026 --> 04:50.656 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting.

04:50.816 --> 04:56.006 align:middle
As the economy evolves, monetary
policy will adjust in order

04:56.006 --> 04:57.856 align:middle
to best promote our
maximum-employment

04:57.856 --> 04:59.206 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:00.556 --> 05:02.126 align:middle
If the economy remains strong

05:02.206 --> 05:04.946 align:middle
and inflation is not sustainably
moving toward 2 percent,

05:05.586 --> 05:07.916 align:middle
we can dial back policy
restraint more slowly.

05:08.386 --> 05:11.586 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

05:11.706 --> 05:14.196 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

05:14.796 --> 05:15.836 align:middle
we can move more quickly.

05:17.236 --> 05:19.906 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

05:19.996 --> 05:21.456 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

05:21.546 --> 05:23.616 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

05:25.656 --> 05:27.346 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

05:27.346 --> 05:30.876 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

05:31.906 --> 05:34.206 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

05:34.586 --> 05:37.286 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

05:37.926 --> 05:40.396 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

05:40.426 --> 05:41.016 align:middle
well anchored.

05:42.186 --> 05:43.336 align:middle
Our success in delivering

05:43.336 --> 05:45.446 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

05:45.856 --> 05:48.766 align:middle
We understand that our
affect-actions affect

05:48.846 --> 05:51.376 align:middle
communities, families, and
businesses across the country.

05:52.676 --> 05:55.186 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

05:56.636 --> 05:58.396 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

05:58.396 --> 05:59.936 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

05:59.936 --> 06:01.046 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:01.996 --> 06:02.656 align:middle
Thank you.

06:02.956 --> 06:04.636 align:middle
I look forward to
our discussion.

06:04.696 --> 06:05.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

06:06.126 --> 06:08.506 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

06:09.356 --> 06:09.906 align:middle
Thank you.

06:10.326 --> 06:13.086 align:middle
Given the expectation that the
election outcome will produce

06:13.086 --> 06:15.856 align:middle
policies that would meaningfully
impact the U.S. economy next

06:15.856 --> 06:17.986 align:middle
year, how is the Committee
taking these proposals

06:17.986 --> 06:19.566 align:middle
into account for
upcoming decisions,

06:19.866 --> 06:21.676 align:middle
including potentially
the next one in December?

06:22.136 --> 06:24.666 align:middle
And, and can you give us any
more sense of how proactive

06:24.666 --> 06:27.966 align:middle
or reactive the Fed is
prepared to be to changes

06:27.966 --> 06:30.076 align:middle
in economic policies with
the next Administration?

06:31.296 --> 06:31.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:31.636 --> 06:35.486 align:middle
Sure. So let me say
that in the near term,

06:35.486 --> 06:38.656 align:middle
the election will have no
effects on our policy decisions.

06:39.116 --> 06:42.596 align:middle
As you know, many, many
things affect the economy,

06:42.596 --> 06:44.666 align:middle
and anyone who writes
down forecasts

06:44.666 --> 06:47.466 align:middle
in their job will tell you that
the economy is quite difficult

06:47.466 --> 06:50.146 align:middle
to forecast, looking out
past the very near term.

06:51.196 --> 06:53.926 align:middle
Here, we don't know what
the timing and substance

06:53.926 --> 06:55.436 align:middle
of any policy changes will be.

06:56.146 --> 06:57.556 align:middle
We therefore don't
know what the effects

06:57.556 --> 07:00.176 align:middle
on the economy would
be-specifically, whether

07:00.176 --> 07:02.156 align:middle
and to what extent those
policies would matter

07:02.156 --> 07:03.936 align:middle
for the achievement
of our goal variables:

07:03.936 --> 07:05.596 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

07:06.496 --> 07:07.786 align:middle
We don't-we don't guess,

07:07.926 --> 07:09.816 align:middle
we don't speculate,
and we don't assume.

07:10.976 --> 07:12.786 align:middle
Now, just in principle,
it's possible

07:12.786 --> 07:15.856 align:middle
that any Administration's
policies or, or policies put

07:15.856 --> 07:18.706 align:middle
in place by Congress could
have economic effects

07:18.706 --> 07:22.176 align:middle
over time-over time that
would matter for our pursuit

07:22.176 --> 07:23.366 align:middle
of our dual-mandate goals.

07:24.126 --> 07:27.006 align:middle
So, we-along with
countless other factors,

07:27.096 --> 07:29.536 align:middle
forecasts of those economic
effects would be included

07:29.536 --> 07:31.586 align:middle
in our models of the
economy and would be taken

07:31.696 --> 07:33.406 align:middle
into account through
that channel.

07:36.126 --> 07:36.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:38.346 --> 07:40.436 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

07:41.486 --> 07:43.426 align:middle
Chair Powell, Nick Timiraos
from the Wall Street Journal.

07:44.146 --> 07:47.806 align:middle
Roughly one year ago when the
10-year Treasury was flirting

07:47.806 --> 07:50.366 align:middle
with 5 percent and 30-year
mortgage rates were near 8

07:50.396 --> 07:52.466 align:middle
percent, you noted how
higher borrowing costs,

07:52.466 --> 07:55.896 align:middle
if they were sustained, could
weigh on economic activity.

07:56.426 --> 07:58.936 align:middle
Given that you've said you
believe policy is restrictive

07:58.936 --> 08:01.156 align:middle
and the Fed is now dialing
back that restriction,

08:01.156 --> 08:02.606 align:middle
are the growth risks presented

08:03.106 --> 08:06.366 align:middle
by higher U.S. Treasury
yields today any different

08:06.366 --> 08:08.676 align:middle
from those you identified
one year ago

08:09.156 --> 08:12.456 align:middle
when inflation was still
meaningfully above your target?

08:14.126 --> 08:15.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:15.666 --> 08:17.556 align:middle
So I would just say this.

08:17.626 --> 08:21.746 align:middle
We've watched the, the run-up
in bond rates, and it's,

08:21.746 --> 08:23.666 align:middle
it's nowhere where it was,
of course, a year ago.

08:23.666 --> 08:24.446 align:middle
I guess that, you know,

08:24.446 --> 08:26.626 align:middle
the long-run rates are
well below that level.

08:26.996 --> 08:27.936 align:middle
So we're watching that.

08:28.086 --> 08:31.676 align:middle
Things have been moving around,
and we'll see where they settle.

08:31.676 --> 08:34.216 align:middle
I think it's too early to
really say where they settle.

08:34.776 --> 08:39.106 align:middle
Ultimately, I'm sure we've
all read these decompositions

08:39.106 --> 08:41.746 align:middle
of what, you know-and
I certainly have,

08:41.746 --> 08:42.866 align:middle
but it's not really our job

08:42.866 --> 08:44.726 align:middle
to provide our specific
decomposition.

08:44.726 --> 08:48.556 align:middle
I will say, though, that it
appears that the moves are not,

08:48.836 --> 08:51.236 align:middle
not principally about higher
inflation expectations.

08:51.236 --> 08:54.206 align:middle
They're really about
a sense of more likely

08:54.466 --> 08:57.096 align:middle
to have stronger
growth and perhaps less

08:57.096 --> 08:58.396 align:middle
in the way of downside risks.

08:58.396 --> 08:59.326 align:middle
So that's what they're about.

08:59.956 --> 09:02.536 align:middle
You know, we do take financial
conditions into account.

09:02.666 --> 09:06.126 align:middle
If they-if they're persistent
and if they're material,

09:06.606 --> 09:09.436 align:middle
then we'll certainly take them
into account in our policy.

09:09.526 --> 09:11.826 align:middle
But I would say we're
not at-we're not

09:11.826 --> 09:12.996 align:middle
at that stage right now.

09:12.996 --> 09:14.496 align:middle
It's just something
that we're watching.

09:14.496 --> 09:18.056 align:middle
And, again, these things don't
really have mainly to do with,

09:18.166 --> 09:20.816 align:middle
with Fed policy, but to do with
other factors in the economy.

09:20.816 --> 09:21.526 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:21.526 --> 09:25.246 align:middle
If I could follow up-is the
September SEP-are those rate

09:25.246 --> 09:26.466 align:middle
projections still valid?

09:26.466 --> 09:30.146 align:middle
Do they still seem relevant
given where we are now?

09:30.146 --> 09:30.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:31.446 --> 09:31.746 align:middle
You know what?

09:31.746 --> 09:33.946 align:middle
It's-you get halfway
through the cycle

09:33.946 --> 09:36.476 align:middle
between the last set
and the next set.

09:36.476 --> 09:38.886 align:middle
I, I wouldn't want to
comment one way or the other.

09:38.886 --> 09:41.826 align:middle
You know, people are-let's talk
about the data we've gotten

09:41.826 --> 09:42.776 align:middle
since the last meeting.

09:42.776 --> 09:47.006 align:middle
So, in the main, the economic
activity data have been stronger

09:47.376 --> 09:48.116 align:middle
than expected.

09:48.286 --> 09:49.776 align:middle
The NIPA revision was stronger.

09:50.296 --> 09:53.156 align:middle
Certainly, the September
employment report was stronger;

09:53.496 --> 09:55.106 align:middle
the October report,
not stronger;

09:55.546 --> 09:56.686 align:middle
retail sales, stronger.

09:56.686 --> 09:59.676 align:middle
So, overall, though, I think
you take away a sense of,

10:00.266 --> 10:03.656 align:middle
of some of the downside risks
to economic activity having,

10:04.056 --> 10:07.906 align:middle
having been diminished, with the
NIPA revisions in particular,

10:07.906 --> 10:11.306 align:middle
and so, overall,
feeling, feeling good

10:11.306 --> 10:12.576 align:middle
about economic activity.

10:12.576 --> 10:14.996 align:middle
So I think we would
factor that in.

10:14.996 --> 10:19.006 align:middle
At the same time, we got
one inflation report,

10:19.006 --> 10:21.556 align:middle
which was-it wasn't terrible,

10:21.556 --> 10:23.426 align:middle
but it was-it was a little
higher than expected.

10:24.006 --> 10:27.186 align:middle
So I think, really, the
question is, is December.

10:27.186 --> 10:29.916 align:middle
And, you know, by December
we'll have-we'll have more data:

10:30.506 --> 10:32.266 align:middle
I guess one more
employment report,

10:32.266 --> 10:34.896 align:middle
two more inflation reports,
and lots of other data, and,

10:34.896 --> 10:37.316 align:middle
you know, we'll make a
decision as we get to December.

10:38.636 --> 10:39.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:41.706 --> 10:43.876 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

10:43.876 --> 10:45.086 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

10:45.206 --> 10:46.966 align:middle
Thanks for taking our
questions, Chair Powell.

10:47.646 --> 10:49.766 align:middle
When it comes to December,
what will you be looking

10:49.766 --> 10:51.826 align:middle
at specifically as you
try to make that decision?

10:51.826 --> 10:54.706 align:middle
And then, as of the Fed's
economic projections

10:54.706 --> 10:56.226 align:middle
in September, you,
you had written

10:56.226 --> 11:00.086 align:middle
down four quarter-point
interest rate cuts in 2025.

11:00.536 --> 11:02.516 align:middle
Do you still think
that those are likely?

11:02.516 --> 11:03.876 align:middle
Is that sort of the
baseline outlook

11:03.876 --> 11:05.436 align:middle
at this point, or
has that shifted?

11:05.436 --> 11:06.516 align:middle
And if it shifted at all,

11:06.516 --> 11:09.106 align:middle
can you-can you give a little
bit of, of detail as to why?

11:09.906 --> 11:10.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:10.296 --> 11:10.786 align:middle
You know, I can't.

11:10.856 --> 11:12.896 align:middle
We don't fill out a-we
don't fill out an SEP,

11:12.986 --> 11:16.586 align:middle
and I can't characterize one
that wasn't filled out today.

11:16.586 --> 11:18.536 align:middle
So I can't really speak
for kind of exactly

11:18.536 --> 11:20.136 align:middle
where the-where the
Committee is.

11:21.006 --> 11:23.926 align:middle
I would just say, for December,
we're going to be-again,

11:24.446 --> 11:27.666 align:middle
in every meeting-we're going to
be looking at the incoming data

11:27.756 --> 11:29.046 align:middle
and how that affects
the outlook.

11:29.046 --> 11:32.076 align:middle
As you know, we're in the
process of recalibrating

11:32.156 --> 11:35.756 align:middle
from a fairly restrictive
level at 5.33 percent.

11:35.866 --> 11:38.406 align:middle
After today's move, we're
down 75 basis points.

11:38.946 --> 11:40.446 align:middle
And we're, we're
asking ourselves,

11:40.446 --> 11:41.686 align:middle
"Is that where we need to be?"

11:42.146 --> 11:44.956 align:middle
You know that we're, we're
trying to steer between the risk

11:44.956 --> 11:49.736 align:middle
of moving too quickly and
perhaps undermining our progress

11:49.736 --> 11:51.466 align:middle
on inflation or moving
too slowly

11:51.466 --> 11:53.206 align:middle
and allowing the labor
market to weaken too much.

11:53.206 --> 11:56.126 align:middle
We're trying to, to
be on a middle path

11:56.626 --> 11:58.356 align:middle
where we can maintain
the strength

11:58.356 --> 12:01.066 align:middle
in the labor market while
also enabling further progress

12:01.066 --> 12:01.606 align:middle
on inflation.

12:01.606 --> 12:02.676 align:middle
We think that's where we are,

12:02.676 --> 12:05.076 align:middle
but that's the question
we're going to be asking

12:05.076 --> 12:07.506 align:middle
in September and, and
in other meetings.

12:07.766 --> 12:09.636 align:middle
And, again, I can't
really update you

12:09.636 --> 12:11.976 align:middle
on the Committee's thinking,
because we don't fill

12:11.976 --> 12:12.736 align:middle
out an SEP at this meeting.

12:13.166 --> 12:13.796 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

12:13.946 --> 12:15.816 align:middle
Yes, total-totally
appreciate that.

12:15.816 --> 12:18.186 align:middle
I guess when it comes to your
own thinking, do you think

12:18.186 --> 12:20.066 align:middle
that that-a full
percentage point of rate cuts

12:20.066 --> 12:21.786 align:middle
in 2025-is a reasonable outlook?

12:21.816 --> 12:22.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:22.626 --> 12:24.476 align:middle
Again, I'm going to
wait-we're going to wait

12:24.476 --> 12:26.486 align:middle
and see how things
come in in December.

12:26.486 --> 12:32.616 align:middle
I mean, I-it's just-I would put
it this way: We're on a path

12:32.616 --> 12:35.506 align:middle
to a more neutral stance.

12:35.736 --> 12:37.236 align:middle
And, and that's very
much what we're on.

12:37.236 --> 12:39.326 align:middle
That has not changed
at all since September.

12:39.986 --> 12:43.526 align:middle
And, you know, we're
just going to have to see

12:43.526 --> 12:45.026 align:middle
where the-where the
data lead us.

12:45.026 --> 12:48.186 align:middle
We have, you know, a whole six
weeks of data to look at to make

12:48.226 --> 12:49.386 align:middle
that decision in December.

12:50.106 --> 12:52.676 align:middle
Obviously, I'm not ruling it
out or in, but, but, you know,

12:52.676 --> 12:59.326 align:middle
I would say-you know, I would
say that, you know, again,

12:59.326 --> 13:02.286 align:middle
we didn't update the SEP, so
I'm not going to characterize,

13:02.386 --> 13:03.456 align:middle
you know, where the
Committee would be.

13:04.946 --> 13:06.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:06.476 --> 13:07.436 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:07.436 --> 13:08.716 align:middle
Thanks. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

13:09.036 --> 13:10.846 align:middle
I wondered if you
could please elaborate

13:10.846 --> 13:13.806 align:middle
and explain a little bit the
two changes in the language

13:13.806 --> 13:17.576 align:middle
of the statement here in the
first paragraph when you say,

13:17.576 --> 13:19.606 align:middle
"Inflation has made
progress," dropping the word

13:19.606 --> 13:23.876 align:middle
"further progress," and in the
second paragraph dropping the

13:23.876 --> 13:26.056 align:middle
sentence that the Committee
had "gained greater confidence"

13:26.056 --> 13:30.046 align:middle
that inflation was progressing
towards its 2 percent goal.

13:30.446 --> 13:32.526 align:middle
Is there any policy
substance behind either

13:32.526 --> 13:34.686 align:middle
of those changes in language?

13:34.686 --> 13:37.396 align:middle
Is it-is it meant to open
the door to a December pause?

13:37.396 --> 13:40.266 align:middle
Is it meant to communicate
anything about the stickiness

13:40.266 --> 13:42.006 align:middle
of core inflation in
the last three months?

13:42.006 --> 13:42.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:42.466 --> 13:43.286 align:middle
Not really, no.

13:43.286 --> 13:46.006 align:middle
So let me tell you
what we were thinking.

13:46.006 --> 13:54.616 align:middle
So the test of gaining further
confidence was a-was our test

13:55.186 --> 13:58.116 align:middle
for the first rate cut, right?

13:58.666 --> 14:01.646 align:middle
And so we, we met that
test in September,

14:02.266 --> 14:04.006 align:middle
and therefore we
take that test out.

14:04.006 --> 14:07.676 align:middle
If you leave it in, then
it's new forward guidance.

14:07.676 --> 14:08.846 align:middle
It's brand new forward guidance.

14:08.846 --> 14:09.606 align:middle
"What do you mean by it?

14:09.756 --> 14:12.186 align:middle
Are you-are you making-are you
requiring yet further guidance?"

14:12.186 --> 14:13.616 align:middle
We have to say "yes" or "no"

14:13.616 --> 14:15.486 align:middle
at every meeting whether
we've made further progress.

14:15.746 --> 14:19.276 align:middle
The point is we have
gained confidence that,

14:19.376 --> 14:21.256 align:middle
that we're on a sustainable
path down to 2 percent.

14:22.016 --> 14:25.266 align:middle
So that, I would tell you, is,
is what that's really all about.

14:25.506 --> 14:27.096 align:middle
And it's not meant
to send a signal.

14:27.096 --> 14:28.966 align:middle
Neither of those is meant
to send a further signal.

14:28.966 --> 14:32.106 align:middle
And, you know, saying "further
progress" it becomes a test

14:32.106 --> 14:34.396 align:middle
where we don't want to be-we
don't think it's a good time

14:34.396 --> 14:37.726 align:middle
to be doing a lot of,
of forward guidance.

14:38.196 --> 14:40.296 align:middle
You know, we're-there's a
fair amount of uncertainty

14:40.296 --> 14:41.496 align:middle
in that-in what I've said.

14:41.496 --> 14:43.186 align:middle
The path that we're on,

14:43.186 --> 14:45.856 align:middle
we do know what-where
the destination is,

14:45.856 --> 14:48.486 align:middle
but we don't know the right
pace, and we don't know exactly

14:48.486 --> 14:50.526 align:middle
where the destination is.

14:50.576 --> 14:55.496 align:middle
So the-the-the point is to find
that, to find the right pace

14:55.496 --> 14:57.716 align:middle
and the right destination
as we go.

14:58.376 --> 15:00.266 align:middle
And I think there's a
fair amount of uncertainty

15:00.266 --> 15:02.456 align:middle
about that, and, you know,
you don't want to tie yourself

15:02.456 --> 15:04.206 align:middle
up with, with guidance.

15:04.206 --> 15:06.626 align:middle
You want to be able to make
sensible decisions as you go.

15:08.366 --> 15:10.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:10.816 --> 15:11.966 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

15:11.966 --> 15:12.626 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:12.626 --> 15:15.686 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, you talked
about higher rates,

15:15.686 --> 15:17.356 align:middle
perhaps from an expectation
of higher growth.

15:17.356 --> 15:18.396 align:middle
You didn't talk about
it in terms

15:18.396 --> 15:20.766 align:middle
of expectations of
higher deficits.

15:20.766 --> 15:23.686 align:middle
Is that something you think
might be behind the recent rise

15:23.686 --> 15:24.546 align:middle
in interest rates,

15:24.546 --> 15:27.196 align:middle
and is that-are rising
deficits a concern to you?

15:28.096 --> 15:28.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:28.476 --> 15:31.386 align:middle
So we don't comment on
fiscal policy, and, again,

15:31.386 --> 15:33.156 align:middle
I don't have a lot
more to say on,

15:33.216 --> 15:34.726 align:middle
on what's driving bond yields.

15:35.306 --> 15:41.626 align:middle
In terms of, of policy changes,
though, let me give you a sense

15:41.626 --> 15:44.086 align:middle
of how this, this works
in the ordinary case.

15:44.596 --> 15:47.826 align:middle
Let's say Congress is
considering a rewrite

15:47.826 --> 15:50.216 align:middle
of the tax laws; it doesn't
matter what's in the content.

15:50.216 --> 15:51.726 align:middle
So we would follow that.

15:51.846 --> 15:54.726 align:middle
At a certain point, we'd,
we'd think we see the outline,

15:54.726 --> 15:55.886 align:middle
so we'd start to model it.

15:56.396 --> 15:59.126 align:middle
And then we'd wait, and we'd
wait, and, at a certain point,

15:59.126 --> 16:02.066 align:middle
the staff would brief the
FOMC and say, you know,

16:02.066 --> 16:03.656 align:middle
"This is-these are
the likely effects."

16:03.656 --> 16:05.656 align:middle
There's lots and lots of
literature on the effects

16:05.656 --> 16:09.656 align:middle
of tax policy changes on
various parts of the economy.

16:10.016 --> 16:12.076 align:middle
So we'd, we'd try to
get smart on that.

16:12.076 --> 16:15.156 align:middle
And then the law actually
passes, and, you know,

16:15.156 --> 16:17.676 align:middle
you'd start to put it-you'd
probably run an alternative

16:17.676 --> 16:19.376 align:middle
simulation before that happens,

16:19.376 --> 16:21.806 align:middle
just to keep people
trying to understand it.

16:21.806 --> 16:24.866 align:middle
Then, then when it actually
passes, it goes into the model,

16:25.626 --> 16:26.946 align:middle
along with a million
other things.

16:26.946 --> 16:29.636 align:middle
So, you know, these-we
have a very large economy.

16:29.636 --> 16:32.006 align:middle
Many things are affecting
it at any given time.

16:32.656 --> 16:36.156 align:middle
And, you know, a law change of
some kind would go in there,

16:36.156 --> 16:37.816 align:middle
but-it would go in,
but it would-you know,

16:37.816 --> 16:39.406 align:middle
it's a process that
takes some time.

16:39.646 --> 16:42.316 align:middle
Clearly, the legislative
process takes a lot of time.

16:42.316 --> 16:44.736 align:middle
And, of course, the real
question is not the effect

16:44.736 --> 16:45.506 align:middle
of that law.

16:45.846 --> 16:48.006 align:middle
It's all of the policy
changes that are happening.

16:48.006 --> 16:51.326 align:middle
What's the net effect and,
you know, the overall effect

16:51.326 --> 16:52.666 align:middle
on the economy at
any given time?

16:52.666 --> 16:55.926 align:middle
So I think that's a-that's a
process that takes a lot of time

16:55.926 --> 16:58.006 align:middle
and that we go through
all the time

16:58.006 --> 17:00.556 align:middle
with every Administration,
constantly.

17:00.556 --> 17:03.376 align:middle
And I just-this will
be no different.

17:03.376 --> 17:04.176 align:middle
But, you know, right now,

17:04.176 --> 17:06.596 align:middle
we're-there's nothing to-there's
nothing to model right now.

17:07.136 --> 17:08.366 align:middle
It's such an early stage.

17:08.366 --> 17:10.076 align:middle
We don't know what
the policies are,

17:10.586 --> 17:11.656 align:middle
and once we know what they are,

17:11.656 --> 17:14.116 align:middle
we won't-we won't have
a sense of, you know,

17:14.116 --> 17:16.966 align:middle
when they'll be implemented or,
or all those sorts of things.

17:16.966 --> 17:18.916 align:middle
So I think I would just
say- STEVE LIESMAN.

17:18.916 --> 17:19.266 align:middle
Mm-hmm. CHAIR POWELL.

17:19.266 --> 17:22.676 align:middle
-we're not doing that now,
and all that will take time,

17:22.676 --> 17:26.776 align:middle
and it will be very much regular
order when we do do that.

17:26.776 --> 17:27.296 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:27.296 --> 17:28.006 align:middle
If I could just follow

17:28.006 --> 17:31.686 align:middle
up on Nick's question-are the
current rates something you feel

17:31.686 --> 17:34.066 align:middle
like you need to lean
against, in that they go

17:34.066 --> 17:36.376 align:middle
against the direction of policy

17:36.476 --> 17:38.886 align:middle
by being-adding restriction
to the economy?

17:38.886 --> 17:40.876 align:middle
Or do you just take them as
a given or perhaps a signal

17:40.876 --> 17:41.926 align:middle
that you should do less?

17:41.926 --> 17:43.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:43.406 --> 17:45.666 align:middle
I-look, I just think-the
first question is,

17:45.666 --> 17:47.016 align:middle
how long will they be sustained?

17:47.016 --> 17:49.016 align:middle
If you remember the
5 percent 10-year,

17:49.576 --> 17:52.666 align:middle
people were drawing massively
important conclusions only

17:52.666 --> 17:54.256 align:middle
to find, you know,
three weeks later

17:54.256 --> 17:57.036 align:middle
that the 10-year was
50 basis points lower.

17:57.416 --> 18:00.736 align:middle
So, you know, it's material
changes and financial conditions

18:00.736 --> 18:03.276 align:middle
that last, that are
persistent, that really matter.

18:03.676 --> 18:05.076 align:middle
And we don't know
that about these.

18:05.466 --> 18:06.596 align:middle
What we've seen so far?

18:06.906 --> 18:08.086 align:middle
You know, we're watching it.

18:08.086 --> 18:08.666 align:middle
We're reading.

18:08.666 --> 18:10.606 align:middle
You know, we're doing
the decompositions

18:10.606 --> 18:14.006 align:middle
and reading others, but right
now it's not a major factor in,

18:14.006 --> 18:16.036 align:middle
in how we're thinking
about things.

18:16.366 --> 18:18.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:20.196 --> 18:21.036 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

18:21.036 --> 18:21.326 align:middle
Thank you.

18:21.326 --> 18:22.936 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

18:24.296 --> 18:26.766 align:middle
You mentioned the positive
economic data that we've seen

18:26.766 --> 18:30.166 align:middle
since the September meeting,
including the revisions

18:30.166 --> 18:32.486 align:middle
to things like savings,
higher GDP growth.

18:33.336 --> 18:35.166 align:middle
We saw a stock market
jump yesterday.

18:35.876 --> 18:37.716 align:middle
That's renewed some of
the questions about why,

18:38.206 --> 18:42.106 align:middle
why do many cuts at all in
this-with this backdrop?

18:42.316 --> 18:43.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:43.446 --> 18:44.396 align:middle
So you're right.

18:44.396 --> 18:46.126 align:middle
As I mentioned, and
as you mentioned,

18:46.126 --> 18:48.246 align:middle
the latest economic data
have been strong, and that's,

18:48.246 --> 18:50.696 align:middle
of course, a great thing
and highly welcome.

18:51.516 --> 18:54.236 align:middle
But, of course, our mandate
is maximum for employment

18:54.236 --> 18:56.716 align:middle
and price stability,
and we think that even

18:56.716 --> 18:59.316 align:middle
with today's cut, policy
is still restrictive.

18:59.686 --> 19:01.386 align:middle
We understand it's not possible

19:01.386 --> 19:02.846 align:middle
to say precisely
how restrictive,

19:02.846 --> 19:04.916 align:middle
but we feel that it
is still restrictive.

19:05.396 --> 19:06.966 align:middle
And if you look at
our goal variables,

19:07.076 --> 19:09.416 align:middle
the labor market has
cooled a great deal

19:09.416 --> 19:11.436 align:middle
from its overheated
state of two years ago

19:11.436 --> 19:13.046 align:middle
and is now essentially
in balance.

19:13.876 --> 19:17.866 align:middle
It is continuing to cool,
albeit at a-at a modest rate,

19:18.446 --> 19:21.066 align:middle
and we don't need further
cooling, we don't think,

19:21.146 --> 19:22.466 align:middle
to achieve our inflation
mandate.

19:22.466 --> 19:23.456 align:middle
So that's the labor market.

19:23.876 --> 19:29.156 align:middle
Inflation has moved down a great
deal from its higher-its highs

19:29.156 --> 19:31.986 align:middle
of two years ago, and we
judged, as I mentioned,

19:31.986 --> 19:34.286 align:middle
that it's on a sustainable
path back to 2 percent.

19:34.706 --> 19:36.166 align:middle
So the job's not
done on inflation.

19:36.166 --> 19:39.476 align:middle
But if you look at those two
things, we judged in September

19:39.476 --> 19:42.436 align:middle
that it was appropriate to begin
to recalibrate our policy stance

19:42.436 --> 19:43.606 align:middle
to reflect this progress,

19:44.056 --> 19:47.816 align:middle
and today's decision is really
another step in that process.

19:48.006 --> 19:49.316 align:middle
Overall, as I mentioned,

19:49.726 --> 19:51.846 align:middle
we believe that with an
appropriate recalibration

19:51.846 --> 19:54.126 align:middle
of our policy stance,
we can maintain strength

19:54.126 --> 19:55.166 align:middle
in the labor market even

19:55.166 --> 19:58.966 align:middle
as our policy stance enables
further progress toward our

19:58.966 --> 19:59.526 align:middle
inflation goal.

20:00.116 --> 20:00.846 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

20:00.846 --> 20:03.606 align:middle
Great. And just to follow up,
could you-what might cause you

20:03.606 --> 20:05.486 align:middle
to pause rate cuts in December?

20:05.486 --> 20:08.336 align:middle
What kind of economic data
would lead you to that path?

20:09.046 --> 20:09.366 align:middle
Thank you.

20:09.526 --> 20:10.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:11.026 --> 20:15.366 align:middle
So we haven't, you know, made
any decision like that at all.

20:15.406 --> 20:17.626 align:middle
But we're-so we're in the
process, as I mentioned,

20:17.626 --> 20:20.146 align:middle
of moving policy down-our
stance down over time

20:20.146 --> 20:21.416 align:middle
to a more neutral level.

20:21.916 --> 20:25.116 align:middle
And, as a general matter, as
we move ahead, we are prepared

20:25.116 --> 20:28.736 align:middle
to adjust our assessments of the
appropriate pace and destination

20:29.216 --> 20:30.396 align:middle
as the outlook evolves.

20:30.746 --> 20:32.556 align:middle
So, for example, if we were

20:32.556 --> 20:34.196 align:middle
to see the labor
market deteriorating,

20:34.196 --> 20:35.916 align:middle
we'd be prepared to
move more quickly.

20:36.696 --> 20:38.956 align:middle
Alternatively, as
we approach levels

20:38.956 --> 20:42.296 align:middle
that are plausibly neutral
or close to neutral,

20:42.376 --> 20:44.826 align:middle
it may turn out to be
appropriate to slow the pace

20:44.826 --> 20:46.416 align:middle
at which we're dialing
back restriction.

20:47.516 --> 20:49.546 align:middle
Again, haven't made any
decisions about that,

20:49.576 --> 20:51.876 align:middle
but that's certainly
a possibility.

20:51.936 --> 20:54.916 align:middle
If you-you can think of it
as similar to what we do

20:54.916 --> 20:57.736 align:middle
with asset purchase-with
asset runoff with QT.

20:58.256 --> 21:00.736 align:middle
So we reach a point
where we slow the pace,

21:01.316 --> 21:04.216 align:middle
much like an airplane reaching
the airport slows down.

21:04.466 --> 21:08.156 align:middle
And so, you know, it-we're
thinking about it that way,

21:08.156 --> 21:09.556 align:middle
but it's, it's something

21:09.556 --> 21:11.926 align:middle
that we're just beginning
to think about.

21:13.286 --> 21:15.456 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:15.996 --> 21:17.286 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:17.676 --> 21:18.046 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

21:18.046 --> 21:22.306 align:middle
So with, with this noise in the
jobs reports that we've seen

21:22.306 --> 21:24.306 align:middle
and you look at the
Fed's favorite inflation,

21:24.306 --> 21:27.246 align:middle
PCE inflation, overall
it's 2.1 percent-very close

21:27.246 --> 21:30.076 align:middle
to the Fed's target-but core
inflation is 2.7 percent,

21:30.386 --> 21:32.176 align:middle
and it's been that
way since July.

21:32.546 --> 21:35.326 align:middle
So why doesn't this
data give fuel

21:35.596 --> 21:37.636 align:middle
to a rate pause for
this meeting?

21:38.906 --> 21:39.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:39.476 --> 21:41.736 align:middle
Well, so I think if
you look at the 3-

21:41.736 --> 21:44.686 align:middle
and 6-month-you're
quoting the 12-month.

21:44.686 --> 21:45.556 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:45.556 --> 21:46.096 align:middle
Yes. CHAIR POWELL.

21:46.096 --> 21:47.356 align:middle
So we look at all
of them, right?

21:47.356 --> 21:48.166 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:48.196 --> 21:48.696 align:middle
Yes. CHAIR POWELL.

21:48.696 --> 21:50.616 align:middle
But if you look at three-
and six-month core PCE,

21:50.616 --> 21:52.006 align:middle
you'll see they're
around 2.3 percent.

21:52.656 --> 21:53.806 align:middle
So we look at all of them.

21:54.096 --> 21:56.486 align:middle
And we look at-we also-we
look at 12 as well.

21:57.006 --> 21:58.406 align:middle
But what it's telling us is

21:58.406 --> 22:00.696 align:middle
that we really have made
significant progress.

22:00.866 --> 22:04.016 align:middle
And we expect there to be bumps.

22:04.596 --> 22:08.686 align:middle
For example, you know, the, the
last three months of last year,

22:08.686 --> 22:10.566 align:middle
the core PCE readings were very,

22:10.566 --> 22:12.446 align:middle
very low-probably
unsustainably low.

22:12.926 --> 22:16.136 align:middle
So that's why you see-that's why
forecasts generally see a couple

22:16.136 --> 22:17.216 align:middle
of upticks toward
the end of the year.

22:17.466 --> 22:20.546 align:middle
On the other hand, the
January reading certainly looks

22:20.546 --> 22:23.466 align:middle
like an example of
residual seasonality,

22:23.696 --> 22:26.556 align:middle
so that we saw last year.

22:26.556 --> 22:30.196 align:middle
So when that falls out of
the 12-month calculation

22:30.196 --> 22:32.116 align:middle
in February, we should
see it staying down.

22:32.116 --> 22:34.376 align:middle
So there will literally be
a bump up and then down.

22:34.756 --> 22:35.646 align:middle
We understand that.

22:36.046 --> 22:39.746 align:middle
Overall, you see the progress
on inflation, and you also look

22:39.746 --> 22:40.706 align:middle
at the economy, and you say,

22:40.706 --> 22:42.536 align:middle
"What's-what is the
inflation story now?

22:42.536 --> 22:43.566 align:middle
Where is it coming from?"

22:43.936 --> 22:45.226 align:middle
So I point to a couple
of things.

22:45.666 --> 22:52.666 align:middle
One is the nonhousing services
and goods, which together make

22:52.666 --> 22:55.756 align:middle
up 80 percent of that-of
the core PCE index,

22:56.286 --> 22:57.726 align:middle
are back to the levels they were

22:57.726 --> 23:01.436 align:middle
at the last time we had
sustained 2 percent inflation,

23:01.436 --> 23:04.206 align:middle
which happens to be in the
early 2000s for a period

23:04.206 --> 23:05.186 align:middle
of five, six, seven years.

23:05.696 --> 23:06.496 align:middle
So they're back at that level.

23:06.496 --> 23:08.176 align:middle
What's not is housing services.

23:08.746 --> 23:10.186 align:middle
So let's talk about
housing services.

23:10.186 --> 23:11.436 align:middle
Housing services is higher.

23:11.876 --> 23:14.796 align:middle
What's going on there is,
you know, market rents,

23:14.796 --> 23:18.096 align:middle
newly signed leases, are
experiencing very low inflation.

23:18.416 --> 23:20.626 align:middle
And what's happening
is older, you know,

23:20.966 --> 23:24.516 align:middle
leases that are turning over are
taking several years to catch

23:24.516 --> 23:25.916 align:middle
up to where market,

23:26.056 --> 23:28.356 align:middle
market leases are-market
rent leases are.

23:28.716 --> 23:30.756 align:middle
So that's just a
catch-up problem.

23:30.756 --> 23:33.676 align:middle
It's not really reflecting
current inflationary pressures;

23:33.676 --> 23:35.766 align:middle
it's reflecting past
inflationary pressures.

23:35.766 --> 23:38.836 align:middle
So that's, that's one thing.

23:38.886 --> 23:42.226 align:middle
The other thing is, I'd say look
at the labor market-not a source

23:42.276 --> 23:43.296 align:middle
of inflationary pressures.

23:43.296 --> 23:44.246 align:middle
Where is it coming from?

23:44.586 --> 23:46.096 align:middle
It's not a very tight economy.

23:46.096 --> 23:48.446 align:middle
What is the story
about inflation?

23:48.826 --> 23:51.556 align:middle
You see that catch-up
inflation also in insurance

23:51.556 --> 23:52.946 align:middle
and in several other areas.

23:52.946 --> 23:56.446 align:middle
So you're seeing-we're not, not
declaring victory, obviously,

23:56.546 --> 23:59.366 align:middle
but we feel like the
story is very consistent

23:59.366 --> 24:02.166 align:middle
with inflation continuing
to come down on a bumpy path

24:02.606 --> 24:05.436 align:middle
over the next couple of years
and settling around 2 percent.

24:05.856 --> 24:08.316 align:middle
That story is intact,
and it won't be one

24:08.316 --> 24:13.006 align:middle
or two really good data months,
or bad data months aren't going

24:13.006 --> 24:14.676 align:middle
to really change the
pattern at this point,

24:14.676 --> 24:16.166 align:middle
now that we're this
far into the process.

24:16.166 --> 24:16.606 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:16.606 --> 24:18.966 align:middle
So you're quickly trying
to get to that neutral rate

24:18.966 --> 24:21.076 align:middle
that you see, or do you foresee

24:21.076 --> 24:22.406 align:middle
that you have some
time to get there?

24:22.406 --> 24:22.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:22.856 --> 24:25.676 align:middle
Nothing in the economic
data suggests

24:25.676 --> 24:28.126 align:middle
that the Committee has any need
to be in a hurry to get there.

24:28.666 --> 24:31.076 align:middle
We are seeing strong
economic activity.

24:31.436 --> 24:33.566 align:middle
We are seeing ongoing
strength in the labor market;

24:33.566 --> 24:34.756 align:middle
we're watching that carefully,

24:35.266 --> 24:37.116 align:middle
but we do see maintaining
strength there.

24:37.706 --> 24:41.346 align:middle
And so we think that the
right way-the right way

24:41.346 --> 24:44.636 align:middle
to find neutral, if you will,
is carefully, patiently.

24:45.336 --> 24:48.566 align:middle
Again, that's not meant to
have a specific meaning other

24:48.566 --> 24:52.036 align:middle
than we-to the extent-to the
extent the economy remains

24:52.036 --> 24:55.856 align:middle
strong, we have the ability
to, to take advantage of that

24:55.986 --> 24:58.426 align:middle
as we try to navigate
that middle path

24:58.426 --> 24:59.436 align:middle
between the two risks.

25:00.896 --> 25:01.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:01.436 --> 25:02.856 align:middle
Craig. CRAIG TORRES.

25:03.506 --> 25:04.606 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

25:04.606 --> 25:05.956 align:middle
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

25:06.196 --> 25:07.786 align:middle
Two questions today.

25:08.296 --> 25:11.526 align:middle
Did you learn anything
about what Americans think

25:11.766 --> 25:14.666 align:middle
about the economy from
the election results?

25:15.336 --> 25:16.546 align:middle
First question.

25:16.816 --> 25:18.936 align:middle
Second question-I want to talk

25:18.936 --> 25:20.796 align:middle
about some labor
market indicators,

25:20.796 --> 25:24.216 align:middle
and I do so with great
respect for your attentiveness

25:24.266 --> 25:28.216 align:middle
to the maximum-employment side
of the mandate, Chair Powell.

25:28.966 --> 25:31.346 align:middle
So the unemployment rate
has been at 4 percent

25:31.346 --> 25:32.696 align:middle
or higher for six months.

25:32.786 --> 25:34.976 align:middle
One of the broadest
measures of unemployment is

25:34.976 --> 25:37.076 align:middle
up about a half a
point from a year ago.

25:37.696 --> 25:39.786 align:middle
Compensation gains
are sliding back.

25:40.216 --> 25:43.326 align:middle
The quits rate, a signal
of labor market dynamism,

25:43.326 --> 25:44.426 align:middle
has been heading down to

25:44.426 --> 25:46.636 align:middle
that bad neighborhood
of the 20-teens.

25:47.246 --> 25:51.566 align:middle
So you have put a marker
at Jackson Hole saying any

25:51.566 --> 25:53.536 align:middle
"further cooling" is unwelcome.

25:53.836 --> 25:56.876 align:middle
It is cooling, generally,
a little bit further.

25:57.406 --> 26:00.066 align:middle
So at what point do we reach
what you would describe

26:00.066 --> 26:02.636 align:middle
as a shortfall from
maximum employment?

26:02.876 --> 26:03.186 align:middle
Thank you.

26:03.406 --> 26:03.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:04.046 --> 26:06.476 align:middle
Sure. So on your first
question, I'm not going to talk

26:06.476 --> 26:08.426 align:middle
about anything that
relates directly

26:08.426 --> 26:10.086 align:middle
or indirectly to the election.

26:10.826 --> 26:14.126 align:middle
On the second one,
you're-you know,

26:14.126 --> 26:15.216 align:middle
this is the great question,

26:15.216 --> 26:16.706 align:middle
and it's the one we
think about all the time.

26:16.706 --> 26:20.006 align:middle
So I'll just say
a couple things.

26:21.186 --> 26:23.386 align:middle
You know, there's nothing
really surprising here.

26:23.386 --> 26:26.716 align:middle
What we know is that the
unemployment rate is low.

26:26.816 --> 26:29.346 align:middle
We also know that it's come
down significantly-sorry,

26:29.346 --> 26:32.936 align:middle
moved up significantly
from a year or so ago.

26:32.936 --> 26:35.326 align:middle
So we've seen a big change
upward in unemployment.

26:35.856 --> 26:38.276 align:middle
Sometimes that has
meant bad things.

26:38.366 --> 26:40.796 align:middle
So far, it doesn't appear to be.

26:40.796 --> 26:42.506 align:middle
It appears that the-I
wouldn't say

26:42.506 --> 26:44.476 align:middle
that the labor market
has fully stabilized,

26:44.956 --> 26:46.396 align:middle
because I do think
it's continuing

26:46.396 --> 26:50.446 align:middle
to very gradually cool, but it
seems to be in a good place.

26:50.446 --> 26:53.066 align:middle
And our policy, of course,
is designed to keep it

26:53.166 --> 26:55.046 align:middle
in that good place, to
maintain the strength

26:55.046 --> 26:57.706 align:middle
in the labor market, while
also enabling further progress

26:57.706 --> 26:58.266 align:middle
on inflation.

26:58.996 --> 27:01.346 align:middle
You know, you mentioned
a bunch of,

27:02.076 --> 27:04.246 align:middle
of indicators, and you're right.

27:04.326 --> 27:07.786 align:middle
You know, the, the openings

27:07.786 --> 27:10.696 align:middle
to unemployment rate is
back to a normal level.

27:10.696 --> 27:13.306 align:middle
I would-I would characterize it
more broadly as "normalizing."

27:13.606 --> 27:14.596 align:middle
You mentioned wages.

27:15.006 --> 27:19.576 align:middle
Wages are still running just a
bit above where they would need

27:19.576 --> 27:24.426 align:middle
to be to be consistent
with 2 percent inflation,

27:24.716 --> 27:27.096 align:middle
unless productivity is going
to remain at this high level.

27:27.096 --> 27:29.976 align:middle
If we see the-if we see
productivity, you know,

27:29.976 --> 27:32.276 align:middle
more sustainably at
these high levels,

27:32.796 --> 27:34.986 align:middle
then that would sustain
higher wage gains.

27:34.986 --> 27:37.826 align:middle
So I would say-in fact, you
can say it the other way, that,

27:38.306 --> 27:40.986 align:middle
that wage increases
are now consistent

27:40.986 --> 27:45.796 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation given
current productivity readings.

27:45.796 --> 27:47.166 align:middle
But, of course, you know,

27:47.166 --> 27:49.866 align:middle
the lore on productivity
readings is whenever you see

27:49.866 --> 27:52.246 align:middle
high readings, you should
assume they're going

27:52.246 --> 27:55.686 align:middle
to revert pretty quickly
to the longer-term trend.

27:55.686 --> 27:57.866 align:middle
That has always been
the case for 50 years.

27:58.376 --> 28:00.866 align:middle
But, you know, it may be
that-we're now five years.

28:00.866 --> 28:03.316 align:middle
If you look at the NIPA
revisions that came

28:03.316 --> 28:07.386 align:middle
out a month ago, we're five
years into, into a nice set

28:07.386 --> 28:10.866 align:middle
of productivity readings, which
are sustained and very healthy.

28:11.336 --> 28:13.496 align:middle
But, overall, it's
a good labor market.

28:13.496 --> 28:15.536 align:middle
We could talk about 20
different data series.

28:16.156 --> 28:19.496 align:middle
We'll be looking at
all of them, of course.

28:19.496 --> 28:23.436 align:middle
But, you know, we don't
want the labor market to,

28:23.516 --> 28:24.726 align:middle
to soften much from here.

28:25.306 --> 28:27.096 align:middle
We don't think we
need that to happen

28:27.096 --> 28:29.396 align:middle
to get inflation
back to 2 percent.

28:30.526 --> 28:30.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:31.286 --> 28:34.936 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

28:34.936 --> 28:36.656 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

28:36.976 --> 28:40.726 align:middle
Some of the President-elect's
advisers have suggested

28:40.726 --> 28:42.406 align:middle
that you should resign.

28:42.686 --> 28:44.816 align:middle
If he asked you to
leave, would you go?

28:44.816 --> 28:45.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:45.436 --> 28:47.116 align:middle
No. VICTORIA GUIDA.

28:47.116 --> 28:50.446 align:middle
Can you follow up on is-do
you think that legally

28:50.576 --> 28:52.266 align:middle
that you're not required
to leave?

28:52.776 --> 28:53.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:54.586 --> 28:57.196 align:middle
No. MICHELLE SMITH.

28:57.286 --> 28:59.176 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:59.176 --> 29:01.316 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

29:01.826 --> 29:06.676 align:middle
You talk a lot about what
the data are telling you

29:06.676 --> 29:09.606 align:middle
and how you are dependent
on the data.

29:09.676 --> 29:14.056 align:middle
But in terms of forward-looking
assessments of the economy,

29:14.056 --> 29:16.776 align:middle
what are you hearing from CEOs

29:16.776 --> 29:18.336 align:middle
or other officials
around the country?

29:18.926 --> 29:21.756 align:middle
What did you hear today from
the regional Bank presidents

29:21.796 --> 29:24.876 align:middle
about what companies
and consumers think

29:24.876 --> 29:27.646 align:middle
about where the economy
is going from here,

29:27.896 --> 29:29.796 align:middle
rather than looking backwards?

29:29.796 --> 29:33.066 align:middle
And does that match up with
what your forecasts have been

29:33.066 --> 29:35.846 align:middle
and what you think the
appropriate policy path

29:35.846 --> 29:36.336 align:middle
should be?

29:36.586 --> 29:37.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:37.236 --> 29:41.596 align:middle
So it's hard to characterize a,
you know, really interesting set

29:41.596 --> 29:43.746 align:middle
of discussions we had, and,
of course, you'll see them

29:43.746 --> 29:45.966 align:middle
in the minutes in three weeks.

29:45.966 --> 29:46.776 align:middle
But I would say this.

29:46.776 --> 29:52.676 align:middle
I think, you know, the comments
from our Reserve Bank colleagues

29:52.676 --> 29:55.606 align:middle
and from the CEOs that they
talk to are pretty constructive

29:55.606 --> 29:57.716 align:middle
on the economy right
now-pretty constructive,

29:57.716 --> 30:01.926 align:middle
feeling that the labor market
is, is, you know, back to normal

30:01.996 --> 30:04.786 align:middle
to the point where it's
no longer that much

30:04.786 --> 30:07.716 align:middle
of a discussion topic in their
world, whereas two years ago,

30:07.716 --> 30:08.916 align:middle
it was all they were
talking about.

30:09.356 --> 30:11.716 align:middle
So they feel like the
labor market's in balance.

30:12.046 --> 30:14.546 align:middle
People feel good about
where the economy is.

30:14.586 --> 30:16.256 align:middle
Demand is obviously
pretty strong.

30:16.826 --> 30:20.396 align:middle
And, you know, you're seeing,
what, 2.8 percent growth

30:20.396 --> 30:21.846 align:middle
in the third quarter estimated.

30:22.126 --> 30:23.026 align:middle
Maybe the year is 2½.

30:24.226 --> 30:27.026 align:middle
This is-this is-you know,
this is a strong economy.

30:27.026 --> 30:31.006 align:middle
It's actually remarkable how
well the U.S. economy has been

30:31.056 --> 30:33.076 align:middle
performing with, you
know, strong growth,

30:33.076 --> 30:35.136 align:middle
a strong labor market,
inflation coming down.

30:35.686 --> 30:39.316 align:middle
We're, you know,
really performing better

30:39.316 --> 30:40.806 align:middle
than any of our global peers.

30:40.966 --> 30:42.136 align:middle
And I think that is reflected

30:42.136 --> 30:47.766 align:middle
in what you hear from-what I
hear people hear from CEOs.

30:47.766 --> 30:50.486 align:middle
I don't get to talk to
a lot of CEOs in my job,

30:51.046 --> 30:53.226 align:middle
but I hear what others
summarize from those.

30:53.226 --> 30:55.306 align:middle
And, of course, I hear the
Reserve Bank presidents do a lot

30:55.306 --> 30:57.646 align:middle
of that, and it's pretty
constructive overall.

30:57.646 --> 31:02.026 align:middle
Now, that's not to say-there
are areas of caution and things

31:02.026 --> 31:04.316 align:middle
like that but, ultimately,
overall pretty positive.

31:04.806 --> 31:05.746 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

31:05.746 --> 31:07.426 align:middle
To follow up on the
areas of caution,

31:07.426 --> 31:10.426 align:middle
if there were black clouds on
the horizon that you identified

31:10.426 --> 31:12.406 align:middle
as something you're
watching, what would they be?

31:12.406 --> 31:13.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:13.036 --> 31:16.956 align:middle
I think it's-you know,
it's things like-clearly,

31:17.736 --> 31:20.236 align:middle
geopolitical risks around
the world are elevated,

31:20.846 --> 31:23.696 align:middle
and just as clearly, they've
had relatively little effect

31:23.696 --> 31:24.646 align:middle
on the U.S. economy.

31:25.286 --> 31:28.946 align:middle
Now, that can change through
the price of oil or otherwise,

31:29.736 --> 31:33.336 align:middle
but people talk about those
as, you know, something that's

31:33.336 --> 31:35.376 align:middle
on the horizon all the time.

31:36.066 --> 31:38.526 align:middle
But, you know, ultimately, if
you look at the U.S. economy,

31:38.746 --> 31:41.376 align:middle
its, its performance
has been very good.

31:41.726 --> 31:45.056 align:middle
And that's, that's what we
hear from businesspeople,

31:45.056 --> 31:46.496 align:middle
an expectation that
that will continue.

31:46.496 --> 31:49.856 align:middle
If anything, people feel
next year-I've heard this

31:49.856 --> 31:53.146 align:middle
from several people-that next
year could even be stronger

31:53.146 --> 31:53.536 align:middle
than this year.

31:55.856 --> 31:56.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:58.076 --> 32:00.016 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

32:00.126 --> 32:02.206 align:middle
Hi. It's Andrew Ackerman
with the Washington Post.

32:02.206 --> 32:03.906 align:middle
I just wanted to follow

32:03.906 --> 32:06.196 align:middle
up on the discussion
earlier on fiscal policy.

32:06.196 --> 32:09.926 align:middle
Your predecessors Greenspan
and Volcker spoke up loudly

32:09.926 --> 32:13.056 align:middle
when they thought large
budget deficits endangered

32:13.106 --> 32:15.596 align:middle
financial-economic or
financial stability.

32:15.816 --> 32:16.996 align:middle
Will you do that, too?

32:17.346 --> 32:20.766 align:middle
And, right now, we're in a
period of full employment.

32:20.766 --> 32:23.926 align:middle
We have large budget
deficits and debt at historic,

32:24.476 --> 32:27.486 align:middle
historic highs that
are-and rising.

32:27.486 --> 32:30.436 align:middle
Is that something you'd
speak out against?

32:30.436 --> 32:30.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:31.066 --> 32:34.836 align:middle
So, you know, I have
said many times no more,

32:34.836 --> 32:39.786 align:middle
no less than what the
predecessors you mentioned

32:39.786 --> 32:40.186 align:middle
have said.

32:40.446 --> 32:44.546 align:middle
And what that is is that the
U.S. fiscal-federal government's

32:44.546 --> 32:49.266 align:middle
fiscal path-fiscal policy
is on an unsustainable path.

32:49.786 --> 32:51.206 align:middle
The level of our debt relative

32:51.206 --> 32:52.926 align:middle
to the economy is
not unsustainable.

32:53.056 --> 32:54.386 align:middle
The path is unsustainable.

32:54.906 --> 32:55.646 align:middle
And we see that.

32:55.646 --> 32:59.256 align:middle
And, you know, you've got a
very large deficit at-you're

32:59.256 --> 33:01.836 align:middle
at full employment, and
that's expected to continue.

33:02.316 --> 33:06.716 align:middle
So it's important that we-you
know, that that be dealt with.

33:06.716 --> 33:08.336 align:middle
It is ultimately a
threat to the economy.

33:08.426 --> 33:09.506 align:middle
Now, I can say that.

33:09.876 --> 33:10.756 align:middle
I don't have oversight.

33:10.756 --> 33:12.426 align:middle
We don't have oversight
over fiscal policy.

33:12.866 --> 33:14.976 align:middle
I've said it on many
occasions-just said it again.

33:16.466 --> 33:17.146 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

33:17.486 --> 33:18.136 align:middle
Okay. Thank you.

33:18.136 --> 33:20.666 align:middle
I guess the only other
question is-to follow

33:20.666 --> 33:24.056 align:middle
up on Victoria's question-do you
believe the President has the

33:24.056 --> 33:25.596 align:middle
power to fire or demote you,

33:25.596 --> 33:29.876 align:middle
and has the Fed determined the
legality of a President demoting

33:29.876 --> 33:31.536 align:middle
at will any of the
other Governors

33:31.536 --> 33:32.516 align:middle
with leadership positions?

33:32.566 --> 33:33.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:33.136 --> 33:33.976 align:middle
Not permitted under the law.

33:33.976 --> 33:34.306 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

33:35.136 --> 33:35.676 align:middle
Not what? CHAIR POWELL.

33:35.816 --> 33:37.696 align:middle
Not permitted under the law.

33:37.756 --> 33:38.926 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

33:39.456 --> 33:39.756 align:middle
Thank you.

33:40.556 --> 33:40.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:41.086 --> 33:41.296 align:middle
Courtenay.

33:41.326 --> 33:41.976 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

33:41.976 --> 33:42.326 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

33:42.326 --> 33:44.336 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

33:44.336 --> 33:45.776 align:middle
In response to Howard's
question,

33:45.926 --> 33:48.876 align:middle
you said it wasn't an ideal
time to give forward guidance

33:49.066 --> 33:50.936 align:middle
because of the economic
uncertainties.

33:51.786 --> 33:54.596 align:middle
Can you lay out what some
of those uncertainties are

33:54.596 --> 33:58.156 align:middle
and whether or not it
includes some of the proposals

33:58.636 --> 34:00.526 align:middle
that the President-elect has put

34:00.526 --> 34:02.986 align:middle
out on the campaign
trail-tariffs, for instance?

34:03.056 --> 34:03.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:03.436 --> 34:05.446 align:middle
No. I was not referring to,

34:05.446 --> 34:08.296 align:middle
to the new Administration's
policies

34:08.416 --> 34:10.936 align:middle
at all, nor will I today.

34:11.206 --> 34:16.366 align:middle
So I-what I'm just saying
is, as we look ahead,

34:17.086 --> 34:18.536 align:middle
we know-and I mentioned this

34:18.536 --> 34:21.626 align:middle
in my statement-that
the risks are two sided.

34:21.886 --> 34:24.766 align:middle
I guess I should start by saying
that we think that the economy

34:25.096 --> 34:26.646 align:middle
and we think our policy are both

34:26.646 --> 34:28.796 align:middle
in a very good place,
a very good place.

34:29.396 --> 34:33.966 align:middle
And-but as you look forward,
you say, "What are the risks?"

34:33.966 --> 34:36.286 align:middle
And one risk is that we
would move too quickly

34:36.886 --> 34:39.706 align:middle
and find ourselves
having moved too quickly,

34:39.706 --> 34:43.766 align:middle
and inflation comes back,
and we-and we lost our chance

34:43.766 --> 34:45.226 align:middle
to get inflation
back to 2 percent.

34:45.226 --> 34:46.406 align:middle
So we have to avoid that risk.

34:46.406 --> 34:48.306 align:middle
And that, that-to
avoid that risk,

34:48.306 --> 34:49.706 align:middle
that means you want
to move carefully.

34:50.286 --> 34:51.956 align:middle
The other risk is that
we move too slowly

34:52.666 --> 34:55.246 align:middle
and that we allow the labor
market to weaken too much

34:55.736 --> 34:58.326 align:middle
and do unnecessary
damage to the labor market

34:58.326 --> 34:59.596 align:middle
and to people's working lives.

35:00.026 --> 35:02.406 align:middle
That, that says, "Don't
get behind the curve."

35:02.406 --> 35:06.056 align:middle
So these are two-these two
things are the two risks

35:06.056 --> 35:06.976 align:middle
that we have to manage.

35:06.976 --> 35:08.576 align:middle
And so we're, we're
in the middle there.

35:08.576 --> 35:10.566 align:middle
We try to be in the
middle and deal with both

35:10.566 --> 35:11.866 align:middle
of the-manage both of those.

35:12.216 --> 35:15.586 align:middle
Again, the idea is to
maintain support, the strength

35:15.586 --> 35:17.526 align:middle
that we have in the labor
market and in the economy,

35:17.896 --> 35:21.226 align:middle
but also with somewhat
less restrictive

35:21.226 --> 35:22.516 align:middle
but still restrictive policy,

35:23.206 --> 35:26.166 align:middle
enable further progress toward
our 2 percent inflation goal.

35:26.336 --> 35:29.576 align:middle
And so there's-you know,
there-this is the thing:

35:29.576 --> 35:32.766 align:middle
Meeting by meeting, we're going
to be making our assessment

35:32.766 --> 35:34.946 align:middle
of what the right path is.

35:35.036 --> 35:38.746 align:middle
You know, it's not as
important-the precise timing

35:38.746 --> 35:41.246 align:middle
of these things is not as
important as the overall arc

35:41.246 --> 35:44.396 align:middle
of them, and the arc of them is
to move from where we are now

35:44.456 --> 35:47.966 align:middle
to a sense of neutral,
a more neutral policy.

35:48.376 --> 35:49.786 align:middle
We don't know exactly
where that is;

35:49.786 --> 35:51.176 align:middle
we only know it by its works.

35:51.776 --> 35:54.566 align:middle
We're pretty sure it's
below where we are now.

35:54.846 --> 35:55.896 align:middle
But as we move further,

35:56.406 --> 35:58.626 align:middle
there will be more
uncertainty about where that is.

35:58.626 --> 36:01.796 align:middle
And we're going to move
carefully as, as this goes

36:01.796 --> 36:04.326 align:middle
on so that, you know, we
can increase the chances

36:04.326 --> 36:05.286 align:middle
that we will get it right.

36:05.286 --> 36:05.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:06.086 --> 36:07.626 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

36:10.036 --> 36:11.166 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

36:11.166 --> 36:12.756 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

36:13.036 --> 36:15.176 align:middle
I know you don't want to
share your decomposition

36:15.176 --> 36:18.116 align:middle
of bond yields, but if you
look at the breakevens, it,

36:18.176 --> 36:21.646 align:middle
it is clear that longer-term
inflation expectations do seem

36:21.646 --> 36:24.936 align:middle
to have risen up at
about 2.5 percent,

36:24.936 --> 36:26.206 align:middle
for example, on the five-year.

36:26.566 --> 36:29.036 align:middle
That's up half a point from
when you cut in September.

36:29.496 --> 36:31.566 align:middle
Do you have any concern at all

36:32.106 --> 36:36.166 align:middle
that longer-term inflation
expectations are deanchoring or,

36:36.166 --> 36:39.216 align:middle
put another way, are anchoring
at a slightly higher level?

36:39.866 --> 36:41.026 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

36:41.026 --> 36:42.886 align:middle
So we, we would be concerned

36:43.446 --> 36:47.866 align:middle
if we saw-if we thought we
saw longer-term inflation

36:47.866 --> 36:49.996 align:middle
expectations anchoring
at a higher level.

36:50.446 --> 36:51.816 align:middle
That's not what we're seeing.

36:51.816 --> 36:53.526 align:middle
We're still seeing
between surveys

36:53.526 --> 36:57.026 align:middle
and market readings broadly
consistent with-you know, I,

36:57.026 --> 37:00.226 align:middle
I looked at the five-year,
five-year earlier today,

37:00.226 --> 37:01.126 align:middle
and it's probably moved,

37:01.166 --> 37:03.826 align:middle
but it's just not-it's just-it's
kind of right where it's been,

37:03.826 --> 37:06.216 align:middle
and also it's pretty
close to consistent

37:06.216 --> 37:07.826 align:middle
with 2 percent PCE inflation.

37:07.826 --> 37:10.596 align:middle
So that's one that's
been a traditional one

37:10.596 --> 37:11.406 align:middle
that we look at a lot.

37:11.456 --> 37:13.886 align:middle
But, overall, expectations seem

37:13.886 --> 37:18.176 align:middle
to be-and really have throughout
this-in a place that's

37:18.176 --> 37:19.986 align:middle
consistent with 2
percent inflation.

37:19.986 --> 37:22.516 align:middle
But you're right to say we
watch that very carefully,

37:22.516 --> 37:27.446 align:middle
and we will not allow inflation
expectations to drift upward.

37:27.446 --> 37:29.996 align:middle
But that's, that's really why
we reacted so sharply back

37:29.996 --> 37:32.666 align:middle
in 2022 was, was to avoid that.

37:34.206 --> 37:34.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:34.526 --> 37:37.816 align:middle
Kelly. KELLY O'GRADY.

37:37.816 --> 37:38.476 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

37:38.476 --> 37:39.906 align:middle
Kelly O'Grady, CBS News.

37:40.296 --> 37:42.896 align:middle
We just talked about what you've
heard from business leaders

37:42.896 --> 37:47.506 align:middle
on the economy, but many average
Americans are still not feeling

37:47.506 --> 37:49.626 align:middle
the strength of the
economy in their wallets.

37:49.786 --> 37:51.576 align:middle
So what's your message to them

37:51.576 --> 37:53.296 align:middle
on when they might
expect relief?

37:53.296 --> 37:54.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:55.346 --> 37:58.316 align:middle
So you're right that, you know,

37:58.316 --> 38:00.646 align:middle
we say that the economy is
performing well, and it is,

38:01.206 --> 38:03.796 align:middle
but we also know that people
are still feeling the effects

38:03.796 --> 38:05.076 align:middle
of high prices, for example.

38:05.716 --> 38:08.336 align:middle
And we went through-the
world went

38:08.336 --> 38:10.206 align:middle
through a global
inflation shock,

38:10.706 --> 38:12.186 align:middle
and inflation went
up everywhere.

38:12.186 --> 38:14.436 align:middle
And, you know, it,
it stays with you

38:14.436 --> 38:16.396 align:middle
because the price level
doesn't come back down.

38:16.946 --> 38:20.976 align:middle
So what that takes is it takes
some years of real wage gains

38:21.266 --> 38:22.336 align:middle
for people to feel better.

38:22.786 --> 38:25.046 align:middle
And that's what we're-that's
what we're trying to create.

38:25.046 --> 38:27.196 align:middle
And I think we're well on
the road to creating that.

38:27.196 --> 38:28.546 align:middle
Inflation has come way down.

38:28.766 --> 38:30.336 align:middle
The economy is still
strong here.

38:30.736 --> 38:33.586 align:middle
Wages are moving up but,
but at a sustainable level.

38:33.986 --> 38:36.106 align:middle
So it's just-I think-I
think what needs

38:36.106 --> 38:38.926 align:middle
to happen is happening and, for
the most part, has happened.

38:39.606 --> 38:42.046 align:middle
But it'll be some time
before people, you know,

38:42.666 --> 38:44.286 align:middle
regain their confidence
and feel that.

38:44.286 --> 38:46.316 align:middle
And, you know, we don't
tell people how to-how

38:46.316 --> 38:47.436 align:middle
to feel about the economy.

38:47.436 --> 38:50.356 align:middle
We respect-completely
respect-what they're feeling.

38:50.356 --> 38:52.056 align:middle
Those feelings are
true; they're accurate.

38:52.506 --> 38:53.506 align:middle
We don't question them.

38:53.506 --> 38:54.646 align:middle
We-you know, we respect them.

38:54.646 --> 38:55.466 align:middle
KELLY O'GRADY.

38:55.556 --> 38:57.796 align:middle
And just a quick
follow-up-President-elect Trump

38:57.796 --> 38:59.726 align:middle
has been critical
of your performance.

38:59.916 --> 39:02.936 align:middle
Any concern about his influence
on the Fed's independence?

39:02.936 --> 39:04.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:04.766 --> 39:06.986 align:middle
I'm not going-I'm not
going to get into the-any

39:06.986 --> 39:09.226 align:middle
of the political things
here today, but thank you.

39:09.226 --> 39:09.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:09.706 --> 39:11.736 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:12.326 --> 39:13.106 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

39:13.106 --> 39:15.356 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

39:15.856 --> 39:21.256 align:middle
What is your plan if we
start to see stagflation?

39:23.666 --> 39:23.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:23.926 --> 39:29.356 align:middle
So that's a-you know, the whole
plan is not to have stagflation,

39:29.356 --> 39:30.346 align:middle
so we don't have
to deal with it.

39:30.346 --> 39:33.876 align:middle
So that's our-that
is actually our plan.

39:34.536 --> 39:36.766 align:middle
[Laughter] You know, it's, of
course, a very difficult thing

39:36.766 --> 39:40.926 align:middle
because you're-you know,
you're-anything you do

39:40.926 --> 39:44.156 align:middle
with interest rates will, will
hurt one side or the other,

39:44.156 --> 39:48.036 align:middle
either the inflation mandate or
the-or the employment mandate.

39:48.036 --> 39:51.326 align:middle
I would just say that, you
know, we've, we've been able

39:51.486 --> 39:55.516 align:middle
to see inflation come down
a whole lot, you know,

39:55.516 --> 40:00.196 align:middle
much closer to our goal without
the kind of sharp increase

40:00.196 --> 40:04.366 align:middle
in unemployment that has
often accompanied programs

40:04.366 --> 40:05.216 align:middle
of disinflation.

40:05.466 --> 40:07.976 align:middle
So knock on wood,
we've gotten this far

40:08.456 --> 40:11.306 align:middle
without seeing a real weakening
in, in the labor market.

40:11.306 --> 40:15.426 align:middle
And we believe we can complete
the inflation task while also

40:15.426 --> 40:17.336 align:middle
keeping the labor market
strong, and that, of course,

40:17.336 --> 40:19.086 align:middle
is exactly what we're
trying to do.

40:19.086 --> 40:19.716 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

40:19.716 --> 40:22.666 align:middle
Can you rule out an
interest rate hike next year?

40:22.776 --> 40:23.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:23.846 --> 40:26.196 align:middle
I-no. I wouldn't rule anything
out or in that far away.

40:26.346 --> 40:28.696 align:middle
But that's certainly
not, not our plan.

40:28.696 --> 40:32.486 align:middle
I mean, our baseline expectation
is that we'll continue

40:32.486 --> 40:35.626 align:middle
to move gradually
down towards neutral,

40:35.706 --> 40:38.646 align:middle
that the economy will continue
to grow at a healthy clip,

40:38.646 --> 40:40.106 align:middle
and that the labor
market will remain strong.

40:40.106 --> 40:42.406 align:middle
If you look at our-look at
our-that will not change

40:42.406 --> 40:44.346 align:middle
from the September SEP.

40:44.346 --> 40:46.446 align:middle
You know, that is our
baseline forecast.

40:46.446 --> 40:48.956 align:middle
And, short of some
exogenous event,

40:48.956 --> 40:50.796 align:middle
it will-that will
continue to be our forecast

40:50.856 --> 40:52.196 align:middle
for the foreseeable future.

40:52.196 --> 40:55.596 align:middle
So-but, ultimately, you know,
it's-we're not in a-we're not

40:55.596 --> 40:58.286 align:middle
in a world where we
can afford to rule,

40:58.636 --> 41:00.896 align:middle
rule things out a
full year in advance.

41:01.586 --> 41:03.466 align:middle
It's-there's just too much
uncertainty in what we do.

41:04.446 --> 41:05.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:05.126 --> 41:07.196 align:middle
Let's go to Jean for
the last question.

41:08.146 --> 41:09.606 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

41:09.946 --> 41:10.626 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:10.626 --> 41:12.206 align:middle
Jean Yung with MNI Market News.

41:12.676 --> 41:15.306 align:middle
I wanted to go back to a
comment that you had made

41:15.306 --> 41:18.126 align:middle
about Americans being
quite unhappy

41:18.126 --> 41:21.306 align:middle
about the cumulative price level
rises over the past few years,

41:21.996 --> 41:24.726 align:middle
even though now inflation is
back on a path to 2 percent.

41:25.676 --> 41:29.006 align:middle
Would it be appropriate for the
Fed to undershoot for a while

41:29.006 --> 41:32.556 align:middle
on its inflation goal under
the average inflation-targeting

41:32.556 --> 41:34.666 align:middle
regime so people have
a chance to catch up?

41:34.666 --> 41:36.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:36.616 --> 41:38.766 align:middle
No. That's, that's not the
way our framework works.

41:38.766 --> 41:42.116 align:middle
We're aiming for
inflation at, at 2 percent.

41:42.116 --> 41:45.016 align:middle
We're-we do not have-we do not
think it would be appropriate

41:45.016 --> 41:47.716 align:middle
to deliberately undershoot.

41:48.976 --> 41:51.346 align:middle
And, you know, part of
the problem there is

41:51.346 --> 41:56.216 align:middle
that low inflation can be
a problem, too, in a way.

41:56.216 --> 41:58.116 align:middle
But that's not part
of our framework,

41:58.116 --> 42:06.136 align:middle
and it's not something we're
going to be-going to be looking

42:06.436 --> 42:15.486 align:middle
at in our framework review.

42:15.486 --> 42:21.066 align:middle
Thank you very much.

